## Findings requiring attention (not covered by the 6 numbered steps)

1. **`get_existing_candles` MCP tool is broken/unusable** — every call timed out with `Read timed out (timeout=10)`, consistently, across the whole session. Worked around by querying Postgres directly (`docker exec postgres psql -U jesse_user -d jesse_db`). Recommend not relying on this tool for the real run.
2. **`get_candles` MCP tool only returns data for the literal stored timeframe (`1m`)** — requesting `1h` or `1D` returns `candle_count: 0` even though the data is genuinely available and used correctly by the backtest engine (which aggregates on the fly). Don't use this tool to sanity-check timeframe coverage; it will falsely report "no data."
3. **Parallel candle imports get rate-limited** — confirms the existing ROADMAP.md note. Only import one symbol at a time for the real run.
4. **A backtest session's `metrics` JSON contained a literal `Infinity` token** (`serenity_index`), which is invalid strict JSON and will crash naive parsers (Python's `json.loads` included). Any automation reading metrics JSON needs to sanitize `Infinity`/`-Infinity`/`NaN` tokens first.

---

## GO / NO-GO Verdict

**CONDITIONAL GO.** All 6 checklist steps executed end-to-end mechanically — data import, backtesting, walk-forward, Monte Carlo, correlation, and reporting all work, and the small issues hit (warm-up buffer, parallel-import throttling, broken candle-lookup tools) all have documented fixes/workarounds above. Execution speed is excellent (1s backtests, 41s Monte Carlo) and will not bottleneck a hundreds-of-candidates run.

**However, one finding is a blocker for trusting results from the real run, not just a dry-run nuisance:**

- **HIGH SEVERITY — Data quality:** ~59% of all 1-hour periods across every symbol tested (BTC/ETH/SOL/XRP) have zero real trading volume — a suspiciously uniform rate across assets with very different liquidity, persisting into the most recent data, not just historical stress periods. This needs root-cause diagnosis (is "Kraken Pro Futures" in this Jesse driver pointing at the right/liquid instrument? Is the OHLC import forward-filling over what should be real sparse-but-nonzero trades?) **before** starting the real 4-year, hundreds-of-backtests research run — otherwise every Sharpe/drawdown number produced would be computed on a majority of synthetic flat-price bars.

**Recommended before starting the real run:**
1. Investigate the zero-volume-hour issue (blocker — see above).
2. Let the XRP import finish (currently running); re-verify XRP's true first-candle date once done, and re-import sequentially only.
3. Push Fold 1 IS windows a couple weeks past the raw first-candle date to leave room for warm-up.
4. Decide how to handle the maker/taker fee gap for limit-order strategies (single flat fee in config).
5. Decide whether to manually model slippage in strategy code, since Jesse has no config-level slippage toggle.
